# Deterministic Finite Automata (DFA) Simulation

## Aim
To write and execute a C program to simulate a Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA) that accepts strings over the alphabet `{a, b}` which start with `a` and end with `a`.

---

## Algorithm
1. Start the program.
2. Read the input string from the user.
3. Find the length of the string.
4. Check whether the first character is `'a'`.
5. Check whether the last character is `'a'`.
6. Verify that all characters are either `'a'` or `'b'`.
7. If all conditions are satisfied, display **"String Accepted"**.
8. Otherwise, display **"String Rejected"**.
9. Stop the program.

---

## C Program

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main() {
    char str[100];
    int i, len;

    printf("Enter the string: ");
    scanf("%s", str);

    len = strlen(str);

    if (str[0] == 'a' && str[len - 1] == 'a') {
        for (i = 0; i < len; i++) {
            if (str[i] != 'a' && str[i] != 'b') {
                printf("Invalid input! Use only 'a' and 'b'.\n");
                return 0;
            }
        }
        printf("String Accepted.\n");
    } else {
        printf("String Rejected.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

